<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>File Descriptor on Many WHYs</title><link>https://wyt1206.github.io/ManyWHYs/tags/file-descriptor/</link><description>Recent content in File Descriptor on Many WHYs</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Sat, 08 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://wyt1206.github.io/ManyWHYs/tags/file-descriptor/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>What Really Happens When Linux Deletes a File?</title><link>https://wyt1206.github.io/ManyWHYs/posts/what-happens-when-linux-deletes-file/</link><pubDate>Sat, 08 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://wyt1206.github.io/ManyWHYs/posts/what-happens-when-linux-deletes-file/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="what-really-happens-when-linux-deletes-a-file">What Really Happens When Linux Deletes a File?&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>When we type:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>rm test.txt
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;p>Most people think:&lt;/p>
&lt;blockquote>
&lt;p>Linux deletes the file immediately.&lt;/p>&lt;/blockquote>
&lt;p>But this is not exactly true.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Linux does not directly erase data.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The real question:&lt;/p>
&lt;blockquote>
&lt;p>What does deleting a file actually mean?&lt;/p>&lt;/blockquote>
&lt;hr>
&lt;h1 id="1-what-happens-when-we-execute-rm">1. What Happens When We Execute rm?&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>First:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>rm test.txt
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;p>is not a kernel operation.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>&lt;code>rm&lt;/code> is a user-space program.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Check:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>which rm
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;p>Example:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-text" data-lang="text">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>/bin/rm
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;hr>
&lt;p>Execution chain:&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>How Does Linux Find the Real Socket From an Integer FD?</title><link>https://wyt1206.github.io/ManyWHYs/posts/how-linux-finds-socket-from-fd/</link><pubDate>Fri, 10 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://wyt1206.github.io/ManyWHYs/posts/how-linux-finds-socket-from-fd/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="how-does-linux-find-the-real-socket-from-an-integer-fd">How Does Linux Find the Real Socket From an Integer FD?&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>In the previous article, we learned:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-c" data-lang="c">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef">int&lt;/span> fd &lt;span style="color:#f92672">=&lt;/span> &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e">socket&lt;/span>(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff">0&lt;/span>);
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;p>returns an integer.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>But this creates another question:&lt;/p>
&lt;blockquote>
&lt;p>If a socket is represented only by an integer, how does Linux find the real socket object inside the kernel?&lt;/p>&lt;/blockquote>
&lt;p>For example:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-c" data-lang="c">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>&lt;span style="color:#a6e22e">read&lt;/span>(fd, buffer, size);
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;p>The application only provides:&lt;/p>
&lt;pre tabindex="0">&lt;code>fd = 3
&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;p>But inside the kernel, Linux needs to locate:&lt;/p>
&lt;pre tabindex="0">&lt;code>socket
TCP state
receive buffer
send buffer
protocol information
&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;p>The complete lookup chain is:&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>